\lispPackageChapter{yahoo-finance}

The package you \texttt{use} from application code.  It re-exports every
external symbol of \texttt{yahoo-finance/rest-api} and adds time
conversion, window normalisation, and \texttt{ohlc-history}.

\section{Time}

Yahoo timestamps are \textsc{Unix} seconds.  Common Lisp wants universal
time.

\lispParameterSubsection{unix-epoch}

Universal time of the \textsc{Unix} epoch, 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.

\lispFunctionSubsection{unix->universal}

Convert a \textsc{Unix} timestamp in seconds or milliseconds to
universal time.  Values above $10^{11}$ are treated as milliseconds.

\lispFunctionSubsection{universal->unix}

The inverse: universal time to \textsc{Unix} seconds.

\lispFunctionSubsection{canonicalize-unix}

Turn a value into a \textsc{Unix}-seconds string for
\texttt{period1}/\texttt{period2}.  Integers greater than $10^{11}$ are
milliseconds; integers greater than $3\cdot10^{9}$ are universal times;
everything else is already unix seconds.

\section{Windows}

\lispFunctionSubsection{canonicalize-interval}

Map a candlesticks short name or a Yahoo interval onto Yahoo's strings.
\texttt{"d"}~$\rightarrow$~\texttt{1d}, \texttt{"w"}~$\rightarrow$~\texttt{1wk},
\texttt{"M"}~$\rightarrow$~\texttt{1mo}, \texttt{"m"}~$\rightarrow$~\texttt{1m},
\texttt{"h"}~$\rightarrow$~\texttt{1h}.  The Lisp reader upcases, so
\texttt{:m} and \texttt{:M} are the same symbol and mean minute; pass the
string \texttt{"M"} or \texttt{:mo} for a month.

\lispFunctionSubsection{normalize-chart-window}

Return a plist \texttt{:interval :range :period1 :period2} suitable for
\texttt{get-chart}.

\texttt{:from}/\texttt{:to} (or \texttt{period1}/\texttt{period2}) win over
\texttt{range}.  \texttt{range=max} with a daily-or-longer interval is
rewritten to \texttt{period1=0} / \texttt{period2=now} so Yahoo does not
downsample to quarterly bars.  With no window at all, daily-or-longer
defaults to that full-history pair.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(normalize-chart-window \&key \lispVar{interval} \lispVar{range}
\lispVar{from} \lispVar{to} \lispVar{period1} \lispVar{period2})}

\section{Bars}

\lispFunctionSubsection{chart}

Alias for \texttt{get-chart}: the raw Yason payload.

\lispFunctionSubsection{chart-result}

Unwrapped \texttt{chart.result[0]} after
\texttt{normalize-chart-window}.  Signals \texttt{chart-error} on an
empty or error payload.

\lispFunctionSubsection{chart-meta}

A plist of what Yahoo said about a result: \texttt{:symbol},
\texttt{:currency}, \texttt{:exchange-name},
\texttt{:full-exchange-name}, \texttt{:instrument-type},
\texttt{:timezone}, \texttt{:exchange-timezone-name},
\texttt{:gmt-offset}, \texttt{:first-trade-date} (universal time),
\texttt{:data-granularity}, \texttt{:long-name}, \texttt{:short-name},
and optional \texttt{:endpoint}.  Mapping \texttt{NasdaqGS} onto a
candlesticks exchange slug is the caller's job.

\lispTypeSubsection{ohlc-bar}

One Yahoo chart bar: as-traded open, high, low, close, optional volume,
and optional adjusted close.  Time is a universal time.  Accessors are
\texttt{ohlc-bar-time}, \texttt{ohlc-bar-open}, \texttt{ohlc-bar-high},
\texttt{ohlc-bar-low}, \texttt{ohlc-bar-close}, \texttt{ohlc-bar-volume},
and \texttt{ohlc-bar-adjusted-close}.  \texttt{ohlc-bar-p} is true of
instances.  \texttt{make-ohlc-bar} builds one from keyword arguments.

This is not a \texttt{candlesticks:candlestick}: there are no
instruments, duration, or retrieval.

\lispFunctionSubsection{result->bars}

A list of \texttt{ohlc-bar} from a chart result.  Bars whose
\textsc{Ohlc} are all null are dropped.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc-bar-tuple}

One ingest row for \texttt{candlesticks:ingest-candlesticks}:
\texttt{(time open high low close volume adjusted-close)}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc-bars-for-ingest}

Map a sequence of \texttt{ohlc-bar} through \texttt{ohlc-bar-tuple}.
This library does not depend on candlesticks; the caller passes the
result into \texttt{ingest-candlesticks}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc-history}

The main entry point.  Returns two values: a list of \texttt{ohlc-bar},
and a chart-meta plist (including the request \texttt{:endpoint}).
Default window is a full daily history via
\texttt{period1}/\texttt{period2}, not \texttt{range=max}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(ohlc-history \lispVar{symbol} \&key \lispVar{interval}
\lispVar{range} \lispVar{from} \lispVar{to} \lispVar{period1}
\lispVar{period2} \lispVar{events} \lispVar{include-adjusted-close}
\lispVar{include-pre-post} \lispVar{cache})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(ohlc-history "AAPL")
(ohlc-history "AAPL" :range "1y")
(ohlc-history :msft :interval "w" :range "5y")
(ohlc-history "AAPL"
              :from (encode-universal-time 0 0 0 1 1 2020 0)
              :to   (encode-universal-time 0 0 0 1 1 2024 0))
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{load-chart-fixture}

Parse a checked-in chart \textsc{Json} fixture under
\path{test-fixtures/}.  Used by load-time specs; default name
\texttt{aapl-chart-5d.json}.

\section{Version}

\texttt{version-string}, \texttt{version-list}, and the three
\texttt{version-*} parameters are re-exported from
\texttt{yahoo-finance/system}.  This is version \texttt{0.1.0}.
